artifacts/WO-RUST-WAVE5-SETUP-01/review.events.jsonl:5:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat OWNER-DIRECTIVES.md && cat AGENTS.md && cat work/WO-RUST-WAVE5-SETUP-01.md && cat work/WO-RUST-SVG-INDEX-01.md && cat work/WO-RUST-DOCUMENT-MODEL-01.md'","aggregated_output":"# Owner directives (highest priority; supersede roadmap/CI/portability wording)\n\n1. **macOS only initially.** Apple Silicon, macOS 13+. No Linux or Windows code, CI jobs, runners, shims, packaging or portability work until the owner lifts this.\n2. Keep platform code isolated behind `platform/` interfaces (clean seams only; no stubs or ports for other OSes).\n3. CI/dev scripts target macOS only. Skip any roadmap item for Linux/Windows (M11, M12) and cross-platform CI matrices.\n\n4. **Full Rust port, owner direction2026-10-14.** Stop the former C implementation queue. Port all first-party application/platform/UI/parser/raster/document/animation/MCP code and in-repo development/test tooling to Rust, then complete the existing product finish line. This supersedes the C17 language/build lock and the former two-implementation-worker ceiling. Use as many useful disjoint Luna codex exec workers as available allowance/resources permit; preserve one writer per module and coordinator-only shared integration. Existing subscription allowance only, no extra credits/API billing. Preserve the current C code, unfinished candidates and evidence as migration references; no delete/reset or false completion claim. macOS13+ AppleSilicon, MPL2, custom UI, owned source-preserving SVG/XML parser/rasterizer, minimal reviewed/pinned dependencies, and no GPL editor source remain locked. Broad UI/E2E at the final stage; focused correctness checks continue.\n# Agent rules\n\nRead [OWNER-DIRECTIVES.md](OWNER-DIRECTIVES.md) first. It overrides conflicting\nroadmap and tooling text. Follow the active `work/WO-*.md`; inspect its allowed\nand protected paths before editing. Keep each work order buildable, tested,\nverified, and recorded in `STATE.md` and the ledgers.\n\n## Platform and product locks\n\n- Initial support is Apple Silicon macOS 13+. Do not add Linux/Windows code,\n  shims, CI jobs, runners, packaging, or portability work.\n- Keep platform implementations behind `platform/` interfaces; do not add\n  stubs for unsupported operating systems.\n- Rust for all first-party code is the active owner-approved migration target.\n  MPL-2.0, minimal pinned dependencies, custom-drawn UI, and an owned SVG\n  parser/rasterizer remain locked. Legacy C17 code is preserved as migration\n  reference; do not resume old C implementation orders. See ADR-0022.\n- Never read, fetch, or copy GPL SVG-editor source. Do not fetch external SVG\n  resources from the application. Record provenance for external algorithms.\n\n## Repository map\n\n- `app/`: executable composition.\n- `src/`: base, platform, XML/SVG, scene, raster, document, UI modules as added.\n- `docs/research-r4/`: authoritative operating model and roadmap.\n- `docs/research-r3/plan/`: architecture and coding guidance where r4 is silent.\n- `docs/adr/`: accepted decisions; `work/`: immutable ready work orders.\n- `tests/`: unit, property, fuzz, UI, and oracle tests as introduced.\n- `bench/`: corpus metadata, generators, runners, and results.\n- `artifacts/`: work-order evidence; `build/` and `.cache/` are generated.\n\n## Commands\n\nThe commands below are the preserved legacy C workflow. Rust workspace/build\ncommands become authoritative only through their implementation orders.\n\n- `./dev build`: configure and build Debug.\n- `./dev test-fast`: run local Python tests and validate the ledger.\n- `./dev verify`: run the Debug build and fast tests.\n- `cmake --preset mac-release && cmake --build --preset mac-release`: Release.\n- `python3 scripts/ledger.py validate ledger.jsonl`: validate work history.\n- `python3 -m unittest`: run Python harness tests when present.\n- `bench`,